== 8. The Outward Mercies of Worldly Men

The eighth #emph[device] that Satan has to draw the soul to sin is by presenting#footnote[It was a #emph[weighty] saying of Seneca (#emph[Nihil est infelicius eo, cui nil unquam contigit adversi]): there is nothing more unhappy than he who never felt adversity.] to the soul the outward mercies that worldly men enjoy, and the outward miseries they are free from, while they have walked in the ways of sin. Satan says, “Do you not see, O soul, the many mercies that such and such people enjoy, who walk in the very ways your soul startles to think of? And do you not see the many crosses they are delivered from, crosses that make other men (who say they dare not walk in such ways) spend their days in sighing, weeping, groaning and mourning? And therefore,” says Satan, “if ever you would be freed from the dark night of adversity and enjoy the sunshine of prosperity, you must walk in their ways.” By this stratagem the devil took those in Jer 44:16-18: “As for the word you have spoken to us in the name of the #smallcaps[Lord], we will not listen to you! Instead, we will do everything we vowed to do: We will burn incense to the Queen of Heaven and offer#footnote[Some of the heathens would be wicked as their gods were, counting it a dishonor to their god to be unlike him. Lactantius.] drink offerings to her, just as we, our fathers, our kings and our officials did in the cities of Judah and in the streets of Jerusalem. At that time we had plenty of food and good things, and we saw no disaster. But from the time we stopped burning incense to the Queen of Heaven and pouring out drink offerings to her, we have lacked everything and have been perishing by sword and famine.” This is just the language of a world of ignorant, profane and superstitious souls in London and England, who would have made themselves a captain#footnote[It is said of one of the emperors that Rome had no war in his days, because it was plague enough to have such an emperor. You#super[+] are wise, and know how to apply it.] to return to bondage, yes, to a bondage worse than the one the Israelites groaned under. “Oh,” they say, “since such and such persons have been put down and set aside, we have had nothing but plundering and taxing and butchering of men,” etc. “And therefore we will do as we and our kings and nobles and fathers have done before; for then we had plenty at home and peace abroad,” etc., “and there was no one to make us afraid.”

#strong[Now the remedies against this device of Satan are as follows.]

The first remedy is to consider solemnly that no man knows how the heart of God stands by his hand. God’s hand of mercy may be toward a man when his heart may be set against that man, as you may see in #emph[Saul] and#footnote[Tully judged the Jews’ religion to be worthless, because they were so often overcome and impoverished and afflicted; and the religion of Rome to be right, because the Romans prospered and became lords of the world. And yet, though the Romans had God’s hand, the Jews had his heart; for they were dearly beloved, though sorely afflicted.] others. And the hand of God may be set against a man when the heart of God is dearly set on that man, as you may see in #emph[Job] and #emph[Ephraim.] The hand of God was sorely set against them, and yet the heart and compassion of God were strongly working toward them. No man knows either love or hatred by outward mercy or misery. For all things come alike to all: to the righteous and to the unrighteous, to the good and to the bad, to the clean and to the unclean, etc. The sun of prosperity shines as well on the brambles of the wilderness as on the fruit trees of the orchard. The snow and hail of adversity fall on the best gardens as well as on the stinking dunghills or the wild waste. #emph[Ahab’s] and #emph[Josiah’s] ends agree in their very circumstances. #emph[Saul] and #emph[Jonathan,] though different in their natures, merits and conduct, yet in their deaths were not divided. Health, wealth, honors, etc., crosses, sicknesses, losses, etc., are cast on good men and bad men without distinction. The whole Turkish empire is#footnote[#emph[Nihil est nisi mica panis.] Luther.] nothing but a crust thrown by heaven’s great householder to his dogs. #emph[Moses] dies in the wilderness, as well as those who grumbled; #emph[Nabal] is rich, as well as #emph[Abraham;] #emph[Ahithophel] is wise, as well as #emph[Solomon;] and #emph[Doeg] is honored by #emph[Saul,] as well as #emph[Joseph] was by #emph[Pharaoh.] Usually the worst of men have most of these outward things, and the best of men have least of earth, though most of heaven.

The second remedy against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that there is nothing in the world that so provokes God to wrath and anger as men taking encouragement from God’s goodness and mercy to do wickedly. You#super[+] may see this by the wrath that fell on the old world, and by God’s raining hell out of heaven on#footnote[Such souls make God a God of rags, one who will not do as he says. But they will find God to be as severe in punishing as he is gracious to others in pardoning. Good turns add weight to unkindness, and our guilt is increased by our obligations.] #emph[Sodom] and #emph[Gomorrah.] This is clear in Jer 44:20-28. The words are worthy of your#super[+] best meditation. Oh, that they were engraved in all your#super[+] hearts, and constantly in all your#super[+] thoughts! Though they are too long for me to copy them out, yet they are not too long for you#super[+] to remember them. To argue from mercy to sinful liberty is the devil’s logic, and such logicians always walk as on a mine of gunpowder, ready to be blown up. No such souls can ever turn aside or avoid the wrath of God. This is wickedness at its height: for a man to be very bad because God is very good. A worse spirit than this is not in hell. Ah, Lord! Does not wrath, yes, the greatest wrath, lie at this man’s door? Are not the strongest chains of darkness prepared for such a soul? To sin against mercy is to sin against humanity. It is bestial; no, it is worse. To render good for evil is divine; to render good for good is human; to render evil for evil is brutish; but to render evil for good is devilish. And from this evil deliver my soul, O God.

The third remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that there is no greater misery in this life than not to be in misery, no greater#footnote[#emph[Religio peperit divitias & filia devoravit matrem.] Religion brought forth riches, and the daughter soon devoured the mother, says Augustine.] affliction than not to be afflicted. Woe, woe to that soul on whom God will not spend a rod! This is the saddest stroke of all, when God refuses to strike at all: “Ephraim is joined to idols; leave him alone!” “Why should you#super[+] be struck any more? You#super[+] will rebel more and more.” When the physician gives up on the patient, you say, “Ring out his knell; the man is dead.” So when God gives a soul up to sin without restraint, you may truly say, “This soul is lost.” You may ring out his knell, for he is twice dead and plucked up by the roots. Freedom from punishment is the mother of false security, the stepmother of virtue, the poison of religion, the moth of holiness, and the bringer-in of wickedness. “Nothing,” said one, “seems more unhappy to me than he to whom no adversity has happened.” Outward mercies often prove a snare to our souls. Ezek 3:20: “I put a stumbling block before him.” Vatablus’s note there is:#footnote[Faciam, ut omnia habeat prospera; calamitatibus eum à peccato non revocabo.] I will prosper him in all things, and not restrain him from sin by affliction. Prosperity has been a stumbling block at which millions have stumbled and fallen, and broken the neck of their souls forever.

The fourth #emph[remedy] against this device of Satan is to consider seriously that what wicked men lack, under all their outward mercy and freedom from adversity, is far greater than all their outward enjoyments. They have many mercies, yet they lack more than they enjoy; the mercies they enjoy are nothing to the mercies they lack. It is true, they have honors and riches and pleasures and friends, and are mighty in power. Their descendants are established in their sight with them, and their offspring before their eyes. Their homes are safe from fear, and the rod of God is not on them. “They send forth their little ones like a flock; their children skip about, singing to the tambourine and lyre and making merry at the#footnote[Men who enjoy all worldly comforts may truly say, #emph[Omnes humanae consolationes sunt desolationes] \[All human consolations are desolations\].] sound of the flute. They spend their days in prosperity. Their eyes bulge with fatness; they have more than heart could wish. They have no struggle in their death; their bodies are well-fed. They are not afflicted like other men,” as #emph[David] and #emph[Job] speak. Yet all this is nothing to what they lack. They lack a share in God, Christ, the Spirit, the promises, the covenant of grace and everlasting glory. They lack acceptance with God and reconciliation with God. They lack righteousness, justification, sanctification, adoption and redemption. They lack the pardon of sin, and power against sin, and freedom from the dominion of sin. They lack the favor that is better than life, and the joy that is inexpressible and full of glory, and the peace that surpasses understanding, and the#footnote[#emph[Nec Christus nec coelum patitur hyperbolen.] Neither Christ nor heaven can be overstated.] grace whose least spark is worth more than heaven and earth. They lack a house that has foundations, whose builder and maker is God. They lack the riches that do not perish, the glory that does not fade, the kingdom that does not shake. Wicked men are the neediest men in the world. Yes, they lack the two things that should make their mercies sweet, namely, the blessing of God and contentment with their condition; and without these their heaven is only a hell this side of hell. When their hearts are lifted up and grown big with the thought of their abundance, if conscience only puts#footnote[A crown of gold cannot cure the headache, nor can a velvet slipper ease the gout; no more can honor or riches, etc., quiet and still the conscience. The heart of man is a three-cornered triangle, which the whole round circle of the world cannot fill (as mathematicians say), but all the corners will complain of emptiness and hunger for something else.] in a word and says, “It is true, here is this and that outward mercy; oh, but where is a share in Christ? Where is the favor of God? Where are the comforts of the Holy Spirit? Where are your evidences for heaven?” etc., this word from conscience makes the man’s face change, his thoughts be troubled, his heart be dismayed, and all his mercies on the right hand and the left be as dead and withered. Ah, if only the eyes of wicked men were open to see what they lack under their abundance! They would cry out and say, as #emph[Absalom] did, “What are all these to me, so long as I cannot see the king’s face?” What are honor and riches and the favor of men, so long as I lack the favor of God, the pardon of my sins, a share in Christ and the hope of glory? O God, give me these, or I die; give me these, or else I will die eternally.

The fifth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that outward things are not what they seem and are thought to be. They have indeed a glorious outside, but if you view their insides, you will easily find that they fill the head full of cares and the heart full of fears. What if the fire should consume one part of my estate, and the sea should be a grave to swallow up another part of my estate? What if my servants should be unfaithful away from home, and my children deceitful at home? Ah, the secret fretting, vexing and gnawing that daily, yes, hourly, attend the souls of those men whose hands are full of worldly goods! It was a good speech of an emperor: “You gaze on my purple robe and golden crown,” he said, “but if you knew what cares are under it, you would not pick it up from the ground to have it.” It was a true saying of Augustine on Psalm 26: “Many are#footnote[#emph[Multi amando res noxias sunt miseri, habendo miseriores.] Augustine.] miserable by loving hurtful things, but they are more miserable by having them.” It is not what men enjoy, but the principle it comes from, that makes men happy. An abundance of these outward things usually brings great distraction, great vexation, and great condemnation at last to those who possess them. If God gives them in his wrath, and does not sanctify them in his love, they will at last be witnesses against a man, and racks to torture and torment a man, and millstones to sink a man forever, in that day when God will call men to account, not for the use but for the abuse of mercy.

The sixth remedy against this device of Satan is to consider seriously the end and design of God in heaping mercy on the heads of the wicked, and in giving them (a #emph[quietus est] \[a discharge\]) rest#footnote[Valerian the Roman emperor fell from being an emperor to be a footstool to Sapor, king of Persia. Dionysius, king of Sicily, fell from his kingly glory to be a schoolmaster. The splendid Queen Zenobia was brought to Rome in golden chains.] and quiet from the sorrows and sufferings that others sigh under. #emph[David] in Ps 73:17-20 shows the end and design of God in this. He says, “When I entered God’s sanctuary, then I discerned their end. Surely You set them on slick ground; You cast them down into ruin. How suddenly they are laid waste, completely swept away by terrors! Like one waking from a dream, so You, O Lord, awaken and despise their form.” So in Ps 92:7: “When the wicked sprout like grass, and all evildoers flourish, it is that they may be forever destroyed.” God’s setting them up is only in order to his casting them down; his raising them high is only in order to his bringing them low. Exod 9:16: “But I have raised you up for this very purpose, that I might display My power in you, and that My name might be#footnote[Valens an emperor, Belisarius a famous general, Henry the Fourth, Bajazet, Pythias, Pompey the Great and William the Conqueror: these, from being very high, were brought very low. They all fell from great glory and majesty to great poverty and misery. #emph[Da Domine ut sic possideamus temporalia ut non perdamus aeterna.] Bernard. Grant us, Lord, that we may so partake of temporal happiness that we may not lose eternal.] proclaimed in all the earth.” #emph[I have appointed you and set you up as a target,] so that I may let fly at you, and follow you close with plague upon plague, till I have beaten the very breath out of your body, and gained a name for myself by setting my feet on the neck of all your pride, power, pomp and glory. Ah, souls! What man in his right mind would be lifted up only to be cast down, would be set higher than others when it is only in order to his being brought lower than others? There is not a wicked man in the world who is set up with #emph[Lucifer] as high as heaven but will with #emph[Lucifer] be brought down as low as hell. Can you think seriously of this, O soul, and not say, “O Lord, I humbly ask that you will let me be little in this world, so that I may be great in another world; and low here, so that I may be high forever hereafter. Let me be low, and feed low, and live low, so long as I may live with you forever. Let me now be clothed with rags, so long as you will clothe me at last with your robes. Let me now be set on a dunghill, so long as I may at last be raised to sit with you on your throne. Lord, make me gracious rather than great, inwardly holy rather than outwardly happy. And rather turn me back into my first nothing, yes, make me worse than nothing, than set me up for a time so that you may bring me low forever”?

The seventh remedy against this device of Satan is to consider solemnly that God often most plagues and punishes those whom others think he most spares and loves. That is, God plagues and punishes them most with spiritual judgments (which are the greatest, the sorest and the heaviest) whom he least punishes with temporal punishments. There are no men on earth#footnote[Ps 81:12. Ps 78:26, 27, 28, 29, 30, 31. Ps 106:15: “He granted their request, but sent leanness into their soul.” It is a heavy plague to have a fat body and a lean soul, a house full of gold and a heart full of sin.] so inwardly plagued as those who meet with the fewest outward plagues. Oh, the blindness of mind, the hardness of heart, the searing of conscience, that those souls are given up to who, in the eye of the world, are reputed the happiest men because they are not outwardly afflicted and plagued like other men! Ah, souls! It would be better for all the temporal plagues that ever came on the children of men since the fall of #emph[Adam] to meet at once on your#super[+] souls, than for you#super[+] to be given up to the least spiritual plague, to the least measure of spiritual blindness or spiritual hardness of heart, etc. Nothing will make that man better, nor move him, who is given up to spiritual judgments. Let God smile or frown, stroke or strike, cut or kill, he does not mind#footnote[It is better to have a sore conscience than a seared one. It is better to have no heart than a hard heart; no mind than a blind mind.] it, he does not heed it. Let life or death, heaven or hell, be set before him, it does not stir him. He is mad for his sin, and God is fully set on doing justice on his soul. This man’s preservation is only a reservation for a greater condemnation. This man can set no bounds to himself; he has become a child of fathomless perdition. He has guilt in his bosom and vengeance at his back wherever he goes. Neither ministry nor misery, neither miracle nor mercy, can soften his heart. And if this soul is not in hell this side of hell, who is? Who is?

The eighth remedy against this device of Satan is to dwell more on the strict account that worldly men must give for all the good they enjoy#footnote[In that day men will give an account (#emph[De bonis commissis, de bonis dimissis, de malis commissis, de malis permissis]) of good things committed to them, of good things neglected by them, of evils committed by them, and of evils allowed by them.] than on the outward good they enjoy. Ah, if men dwelt more on the account they must give before long for all the mercies they have enjoyed, and for all the favors they have abused, and for all the sins they have committed, it would make their hearts tremble and their lips quiver, and rottenness enter their bones. It would cause their souls to cry out and say, “Oh, that our mercies had been fewer and smaller, so that our account might have been easier, and our torment and misery (for our abuse of so great mercy) not greater than we are able to bear! Oh, cursed be the day when the crown of honor was set on our heads, and the treasures of this world were cast into our laps! Oh, cursed be the day when the sun of prosperity shone so strongly on us, and this flattering world smiled so much on us, as to lead us to forget God, to slight Jesus Christ, to neglect our souls, and to put far from us the day of our account!” #emph[Philip] the Third of #emph[Spain,] whose life was free from gross evils, professed that he would rather lose all his kingdom than offend God willingly. Yet in the agony of death, considering more thoroughly the#footnote[#emph[In die judicii plus valebit conscientia pura, quam marsupia plena.] Bernard. Then a good conscience will be worth more than all the world’s goods.] account he was to give to God, fear struck into him, and these words burst from him: “Oh, would to God I had never reigned! Oh, that in those years I have spent in my kingdom I had lived a life in the wilderness! Oh, that I had lived a solitary life with God! How much more securely I would now die! How much more confidently I would go to the throne of God! What does all my glory profit me, except that I have so much the more torment in my death?” God keeps an exact account of every penny that is laid out on him and his, and that is laid out against him and his. And men will know and feel this in the day of account, though now they shut their eyes and#footnote[Jerome always thought that that voice was in his ears (#emph[Surgite mortui & venite ad judicium]): “Arise, you#super[+] dead, and come to judgment.” As often as I think of that day, how my whole body quakes, and my heart within me trembles!] will not understand. The sleeping of vengeance causes the overflowing of sin, and the overflow of sin causes the awakening of vengeance. Abused mercy will certainly turn into fury. God’s forbearance is no acquittal. The day is at hand when he will pay wicked men for the abuse of new and old mercies. If he seems to be slow, yet he is sure: he has leaden heels but iron hands. The farther back he swings his blow, or draws his arrow, the deeper he will wound in the day of vengeance. Men’s actions are all in print in heaven, and God will, in the day of account, read them aloud in the ears of all the world, so that they may all say amen to the righteous sentence he will pass on all the despisers and abusers of mercy.
